# CLRI(1M)

## NAME

clri - clear i-node

## SYNOPSIS

| **/etc/clri** *filesystem i-number ...*

## DESCRIPTION

*Clri* writes zeros on the i-nodes with the decimal *i-numbers* on the *filesystem.* After
*clri,* any blocks in the affected file will show up as `missing` in an *icheck*(1M) of the
*filesystem.*

Read and write permission is required on the specified file system device. The i-node becomes
allocatable.

The primary purpose of this routine is to remove a file which for some reason appears in no
directory. If it is used to zap an i-node which does appear in a directory, care should be
taken to track down the entry and remove it. Otherwise, when the i-node is reallocated to some
new file, the old entry will still point to that file. At that point removing the old entry
will destroy the new file. The new entry will again point to an unallocated i-node, so the
whole cycle is likely to be repeated again and again.

**Note:** *fsck*(1M) is the modern way to do all of that. It finds a file that appears in no
directory for itself and offers to reconnect it into **/lost+found**, and it removes an entry
naming an unallocated i-node rather than leaving the cycle above to be broken by hand. *Clri*
is worth reaching for when the file is to be thrown away rather than kept.

Every *i-number* is checked, and the block it lies in read, before **anything** is written: a
run that is going to fail on its fourth argument fails before it has cleared the first three.

**Note:** an *i-number* outside the file system's i-list is **refused**. v7 seeks wherever the
number names, so **clri /dev/rmd0 99999** there writes 3072 bytes of zeros over a **data**
block - somebody's file - with no diagnostic at all. This version reads the super-block, works
out how many i-numbers the volume has, and refuses anything outside 2 through that. I-node 1
goes with them: it exists and *ialloc*() can never hand it out.

**Note:** if the *filesystem* is the mounted root, *clri* prints **\*\*\*\*\* BOOT UNIX (NO
SYNC!) \*\*\*\*\*** and stops, as *fsck*(1M) does. That is what the BUGS section below means in
this system's terms.

## FILES

/dev/rmd0, /dev/rmd1

## SEE ALSO

icheck(1M), dcheck(1M), ncheck(1M), fsck(1M), filsys(5)

## DIAGNOSTICS

`arg: is not a number` and `arg: is zero` are v7's. `arg: out of range 2..n` is the check
above.

`filesystem: not a filesystem` means the super-block carries no *FS_MAGIC* and `filesystem
geometry mismatch` that it describes a block size, an i-nodes-per-block or an
addresses-per-inode this system does not have. Either way there is no i-list to bound an
*i-number* against, so nothing is cleared.

## BUGS

If the file is open, *clri* is likely to be ineffective.

**Note:** specifically, the kernel holds the i-node in core while the file is open and *iput*()
writes that copy back to the disk when the last reference goes away - over the zeros this
program wrote. The same thing on a larger scale is why the mounted root stops the machine
rather than returning: the whole in-core i-node cache is newer than the disk.
